Cell["\<\
There are 4 possible sets of 3 groups that involve the 4 input variables \
raised to the first power.   The number can, in this case, be determined from \
binomial function, 4 choose 3, which is equal to 4.  However, in general, the \
number may in some cases smaller than this, because it is possible that some \
of the cases do not result in independent Pi groups.  BuckPi works this out \
for you.

The second optional argument to BuckPi will specify that we only want certain \
groups.  The argument is given as a list of quantities, referred to by their \
position in the variable list.  BuckPi will restrict the output, if possible, \
to involve only those forms of the groups which involve the specified \
variables raised to the first power.  For example, if we only want V raised \
to the first power in the example above, we write\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.937412397398259*^9, 3.937412523144978*^9}, {
  3.937412566048688*^9, 3.937412653220837*^9}, {3.9374134772824087`*^9, 
  3.9374134789576807`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6d1b5e14-d16a-49b9-b5e6-\
190cb2e7c63a"],

