Cell["\<\
Thus in the above, BuckPi has ignored the 4th variable.

In general, it is possible that does not exist a valid set of Pi groups when \
various parameters are constrained to appear to the first power.  In this \
problem, all combinations of 3 of the 4 variables are valid, as shown in the \
first example.

The third optional argument is a Boolean variable that controls the form of \
the output.  The default (True) makes a pretty table, whereas setting this \
argument to False will return a table in standard output form (so that it can \
easily be manipulated).
\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.937412836227901*^9, 3.937412937852976*^9}, {
  3.93741311297823*^9, 
  3.9374131814250402`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e7da721a-e34e-4e09-a680-\
ee357e8f85aa"],

